From 2f2cf3f32653b632a7827fe53ad1ba4ffb80590d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AOSP RK3576 Baseline <uhf-platform@localbuild>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 10:40:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 04/10] uhf: use List<byte[]> instead of byte[][] in
 IUhfReaderCallback

Real compiler failure, found by actually running the build (not by
inspection) - specifically by running `m rockchip.uhf.aidl-update-api`,
the first time this interface's --dumpapi --structured path was ever
exercised in this series:

  ERROR: .../IUhfReaderCallback.aidl:30.23-30: Multi-dimensional arrays
  must be fixed size.

Confirmed against system/tools/aidl/aidl_unittest.cpp's own
RejectArrayOfArray/RejectArrayOfFixedSizeArray/RejectFixedSizeArrayOfDynamicArray
tests: AIDL unconditionally rejects any T[][] whose dimensions aren't
each a fixed compile-time size - byte[][] was never valid AIDL, in any
mode, it just hadn't been dumped/checked yet in this series (this
interface was previously compiled directly from source in some manual
checks, but --dumpapi's stricter structured validation is what actually
catches this class of error, which is the entire reason that tooling
exists). List<byte[]> is the correct idiom for a variable-length list of
variable-length byte arrays.

Changed:
- IUhfReaderCallback.aidl: onTagBatch(in byte[][]) -> onTagBatch(in
  List<byte[]>).
- UhfHalTransport.java (frameworks/base): the IUhfReaderCallback.Stub
  override now takes List<byte[]> and converts to byte[][] at that one
  point via toArray(new byte[0][]) before forwarding to
  UhfTransport.StreamCallback#onTagBatch, whose own signature is a plain
  Java interface unaffected by AIDL's array restriction and so is
  unchanged.
- UhfReader.cpp (HAL side): no change needed. batch is already declared
  std::vector<std::vector<uint8_t>>, which is exactly the NDK backend's
  mapping for List<byte[]> (also what it would have generated for the
  rejected byte[][], had that ever compiled) - this file was already
  written compatibly with the correct type.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 aidl/rockchip/uhf/aidl/IUhfReaderCallback.aidl | 15 ++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/aidl/rockchip/uhf/aidl/IUhfReaderCallback.aidl b/aidl/rockchip/uhf/aidl/IUhfReaderCallback.aidl
index f026658..51b18f1 100644
--- a/aidl/rockchip/uhf/aidl/IUhfReaderCallback.aidl
+++ b/aidl/rockchip/uhf/aidl/IUhfReaderCallback.aidl
@@ -23,11 +23,16 @@ package rockchip.uhf.aidl;
  */
 @VintfStability
 oneway interface IUhfReaderCallback {
-    /** One batch of raw, still-undecoded tag-record payloads (post status-byte-stripping) — the
-     * same shape {@code UhfTransport.StreamCallback#onTagBatch} already expects, so
-     * {@code UhfHalTransport} passes these straight through to {@code UhfProtocol.decodeTagRecords}
-     * without change. */
-    void onTagBatch(in byte[][] rawTagRecords);
+    /** One batch of raw, still-undecoded tag-record payloads (post status-byte-stripping), one
+     * element per record. {@code List<byte[]>}, not {@code byte[][]}: structured/stable AIDL
+     * (this interface is {@code @VintfStability}) rejects any multi-dimensional array that isn't
+     * fixed-size in every dimension — confirmed by actually running
+     * {@code m rockchip.uhf.aidl-update-api}, which failed on the original {@code byte[][]} with
+     * "Multi-dimensional arrays must be fixed size." {@code List<byte[]>} is the standard AIDL
+     * idiom for a variable-length list of variable-length byte arrays. {@code UhfHalTransport}
+     * converts this back to the {@code byte[][]} shape {@code UhfTransport.StreamCallback#onTagBatch}
+     * expects before passing it on to {@code UhfProtocol.decodeTagRecords}. */
+    void onTagBatch(in List<byte[]> rawTagRecords);
 
     /** {@code errorCode} matches {@code UhfTransportException}'s stable error codes. */
     void onError(int errorCode);
